The accelerated electrons emit synchrotron radiation in the local magnetic field. For the full synchrotron emissivity we use the analytical expressions of Blumenthal and Gould (1970) for both electrons and protons, although the proton synchrotron component is generally subdominant for the parameters considered here ( Muecke et al., 2003 ) . The same non-thermal electrons inverse-Compton scatter the synchrotron photons, disk photons, and coronal photons. We include the transition from the Thomson to the Klein–Nishina regime in the calculation of the emitted spectrum and in the cooling time-scale. This leptonic channel is particularly important in the GeV band, especially when the magnetic field is weak enough for inverse Compton cooling to compete with synchrotron cooling. Relativistic protons interact with both gas and radiation. Inelastic proton-proton collisions occur on the thermal protons of the corona, disk atmosphere, or debris environment, while photohadronic interactions occur on the synchrotron, disk, and coronal photon fields. These interactions produce charged and neutral pions. Neutral pions decay into γ \gamma rays, while charged pions decay into neutrinos and secondary pairs. We use the semi-analytical prescription of Kelner et al. (2006) , with the updated cross sections of Kafexhiu et al. (2014) , for pp interactions and the prescription of Kelner and Aharonian (2008) for p γ \gamma interactions. The resulting secondary electrons and positrons are followed radiatively, allowing electromagnetic cascades to redistribute part of the hadronic power to lower photon energies. The neutrino spectrum is calculated consistently with the charged-pion component of the pp and p γ \gamma interactions. At production, the flavor composition is dominated by the pion-decay chain, while at Earth we assume standard flavor mixing, leading approximately to equal fluxes of electron, muon, and tau neutrinos. For a source at luminosity distance d L d_{L} , the all-flavor neutrino energy flux is obtained from the intrinsic neutrino luminosity through the usual geometric dilution. To estimate the number of detectable events in KM3NeT/ARCA, the model flux can then be folded with the declination- and energy-dependent effective area, N ν = Δ ​ t ​ ∑ α ∫ d ​ E ν ​ A eff , α ​ ( E ν , δ ) ​ ϕ ν α ​ ( E ν ) , N_{
