In Figs. 1 – 4 , we show representative intrinsic spectral energy distributions (SEDs) to illustrate how the main model parameters affect the emitted radiation before attenuation. These curves are not intended as fits to a specific TDE. They are used to identify the regions of parameter space in which TDEs may become detectable in the GeV–TeV band and in neutrinos. Figure 1 shows the effect of the thermal photon fields. In the left panel, we vary the disk luminosity while keeping the disk truncation radius fixed at 10 ​ R g 10R_{g} . A more luminous disk naturally increases the optical/UV emission and also enhances the external photon field available for inverse Compton scattering and p γ \gamma interactions. In the right panel, we include a thermal corona with τ cor = 0.5 	au_{m cor}=0.5 and vary k B ​ T cor k_{m B}T_{m cor} . A hotter corona upscatters disk photons to higher energies and increases the density of X-ray target photons. The remaining parameters are fixed to a fiducial model in which a dusty torus with luminosity 6 × 10 39 6	imes 10^{39} erg s -1 dominates the IR band, non-thermal electrons are injected from a Maxwell-Jüttner distribution peaking at 50 keV, the electron power-law index is p e = 2 p_{e}=2 , the shocks are located at 20 ​ R g 20R_{g} , f sc = 10 − 6 f_{m sc}=10^{-6} , and β = 1  eta=1 . Figure 2 isolates the leptonic component in the absence of a corona. The viewing angle affects the observed normalization because the emission is preferentially released along the axis perpendicular to the disk plane. The plasma beta controls the balance between synchrotron and inverse Compton radiation: near equipartition, the stronger magnetic field enhances synchrotron emission, whereas larger β  eta values suppress synchrotron cooling and can increase the relative importance of inverse Compton scattering. The acceleration efficiency f sc f_{m sc} controls the maximum particle energy and therefore the high-energy cutoff. Finally, harder electron 