u}_{\mu} spectra for Models B–D are shown in Fig. 21 . Model B gives a comparable, and in parts higher, neutrino flux despite the absence of a corona. In this case, the hard proton spectrum and efficient acceleration allow the source to remain neutrino bright while also becoming more transparent to escaping GeV-to-TeV photons. Models C and D show that soft proton spectra can also approach the KM3NeT benchmark, but only under more specific conditions. In Model C, the large value of γ p , min \gamma_{p,m min} removes low-energy protons that do not efficiently contribute to pion production and concentrates the injected power closer to the relevant energy range. In Model D, the same goal is achieved by increasing the non-thermal proton fraction. Model E is not shown in Fig. 21 , because its high-energy output is optimised for the GeV band and the corresponding neutrino flux is negligible on this scale. As in the case of AT2019dsg, the comparison with the grey ARCA230 curve should be used only as a guide. The grey line is a ten-year steady point-source sensitivity, whereas the neutrino emission from a TDE is transient. The final detectability of each model must therefore be evaluated through the event-count calculation using the source position, the assumed active time window, and the flavour-dependent effective area.

